-- title:
--   Cayley port graphs of finite symmetric groups and their all-colourings disjoint union
-- statement:
--   Let $A$ be a finite set, $F(A)$ the free group on $A$, and $N\in\mathbb{N}$. Let $B_N=\{g\in F(A):|g|\le N\}$ be the ball of reduced words of length at most $N$, and $\rho_N:F(A)\to\mathrm{Sym}(B_N)$ the finite permutation representation in which each generator $a$ acts by a fixed permutation of $B_N$ agreeing with $g\mapsto a\,g$ whenever $|a\,g|\le N$. Put $\Gamma_N=\mathrm{Sym}(B_N)$, the finite group of all permutations of $B_N$.
--
--   1. **Ports and letters.** The port set is $A\times\{\mathrm{true},\mathrm{false}\}$. The letter of a port is $\operatorname{letter}(a,\mathrm{true})=a$ and $\operatorname{letter}(a,\mathrm{false})=a^{-1}$ in $F(A)$. Reversing a port flips its Boolean component, $(a,b)\mapsto(a,\neg b)$, and the letter of the reversed port is the inverse letter.
--   2. **Cayley port graph.** The port graph with vertex set $\Gamma_N$, these ports and this reversal has moves
--   $$x\cdot e=x\;\rho_N\bigl(\operatorname{letter}(e)\bigr)\qquad(x\in\Gamma_N),$$
--   the product being taken in $\Gamma_N$; moving along $e$ and then along its reverse returns to $x$. (Its connected components are the left cosets of the subgroup generated by $\rho_N(A)$.)
--   3. **All colourings.** For a set $C$ of colours, the port graph with vertex set $C^{\Gamma_N}\times\Gamma_N$, whose vertices are pairs $(c,x)$ of a colouring $c:\Gamma_N\to C$ and a vertex $x$, has the same ports and the moves
--   $$(c,x)\cdot e=(c,\;x\cdot e).$$
--   It is the disjoint union of copies of the Cayley port graph, one for each colouring of $\Gamma_N$.
--
--   These graphs are the large-girth port graphs of the construction: since $\rho_N$ reproduces the action of $F(A)$ on short words, a reduced walk of length at most $N$ in the Cayley port graph visits pairwise distinct vertices, so the graph looks like the tree of the free group at that scale. Taking all colourings at once lets counting arguments range over every colouring of the same graph.
--
--   **Formalization Note** `Cayley A N` is the `abbrev` `Equiv.Perm (WordBall A N)`; ports are `A × Bool`, with `invertPort`, `letter` and `letter_invert`; `cayleyGraph A N` and `allColoringsGraph A C N` are `PortGraph` structures. In Mathlib the product of permutations is composition, $(\sigma\tau)(g)=\sigma(\tau(g))$.
